{\bfseries \fontsize{14}{16}\selectfont \centerline{Abstract} \vspace*{1.5\baselineskip}}
\setlength{\parindent}{11mm}

Modern retail and e-commerce merchants frequently list identical Stock Keeping Units (SKUs) simultaneously across multiple independent sales channels, including online marketplaces such as Amazon and Flipkart, direct-to-consumer (D2C) web storefronts, and physical point-of-sale (POS) shop counters. Because each sales channel operates as an isolated system with its own local view of inventory, keeping stock counts synchronized in real time is a critical operational challenge. Existing budget-friendly inventory tools rely on periodic polling intervals of 15 to 30 minutes and destructive state-overwrite updates. This architectural limitation creates five persistent failure modes: synchronization blind spots between polling cycles, concurrent race conditions when two channels sell the final unit simultaneously, double-deduction of stock caused by duplicate webhook retries during network latency spikes, naive first-come-first-served or negative-stock failure states during stock-outs, and complete absence of a permanent audit trail explaining why a stock discrepancy occurred. Conversely, enterprise Order Management Systems (OMS) such as Cin7 require expensive monthly subscriptions (\$350 to \$1,000+ per month) and rely on rigid, manually configured static rules, while standalone profit-analytics platforms (such as TrueProfit) operate in isolation from real-time inventory allocation decisions.

To bridge this gap for small and mid-scale multi-channel sellers, this project presents the \textbf{Multi-Channel Inventory Reconciliation Engine}, a fault-tolerant, event-sourced distributed system engineered to maintain a single, verifiable source of truth across heterogeneous sales channels. Instead of overwriting a single mutable stock counter, the engine applies the \textit{Event Sourcing} architectural pattern: every sale, return, restock, and cancellation is permanently recorded as an immutable domain event in a PostgreSQL ledger, and current stock is derived deterministically as the cumulative fold of all historical events. Incoming channel webhooks are load-balanced through Nginx across three horizontally scaled Node.js and Express intake nodes, deduplicated in sub-millisecond time via atomic idempotency keys in Redis, and persisted into a durable RabbitMQ message queue to guarantee zero event loss even under server crashes.

When a Python reconciliation worker detects that concurrent sales across channels would drive stock below zero, the system executes a smart, priority-based conflict resolution algorithm. Rather than blindly rejecting an order or allowing negative stock, the engine evaluates competing orders using business-critical metrics—specifically net profit margin, customer importance, and channel cancellation penalties—to recommend or automatically unwind the least costly order while immediately notifying the affected customer and logging a permanent audit record. Furthermore, the project introduces a genuine architectural innovation absent from existing basic, enterprise, and analytics tools: an \textbf{adaptive, human-in-the-loop recommendation loop}. By continuously analyzing its own immutable history of stock-out conflicts and margin losses, the engine proactively surfaces evidence-based safety buffer recommendations (for example, holding back specific units on high-conflict channels) on a live HTML/JavaScript seller dashboard. Crucially, a human operator always approves or rejects each suggestion before any allocation policy changes, combining automated operational intelligence with strict human financial accountability.

