\section{Problem Statement}
\label{sec:problem}
To design and implement a centralized, fault-tolerant \textbf{Multi-Channel Inventory Reconciliation Engine} based on immutable Event Sourcing, Redis-backed idempotency deduplication, RabbitMQ durable message queuing, and Python-based priority conflict resolution that eliminates multi-channel overselling across independent retail platforms (Amazon, Flipkart, direct web store, and physical POS counter), preserves a complete audit trail of every stock mutation, provides integrated order-level profit analytics, and proactively generates evidence-based, human-approved inventory buffer recommendations from historical conflict patterns.

\section{Abstract}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Multi-channel sellers listing identical inventory across Amazon, Flipkart, direct web stores, and physical counters frequently suffer from overselling when concurrent sales occur within seconds of one another.
  \item Conventional budget tools poll channels every 15--30 minutes and overwrite stock numbers in place, resulting in sync blind spots, race conditions, duplicate webhook deductions, naive order rejections, and zero historical auditability.
  \item The proposed system replaces state-overwrite storage with an immutable \textbf{Event Sourcing} ledger in PostgreSQL where current stock is computed as the exact mathematical fold of all recorded sales, returns, restocks, and cancellations.
  \item Incoming webhook traffic is distributed by Nginx across three Node.js + Express intake nodes, checked for duplicates in sub-millisecond time via Redis, and persisted in a durable RabbitMQ queue for crash-resilient processing.
  \item When concurrent last-unit sales trigger a negative-stock conflict, a Python reconciliation worker evaluates order profit margins and customer value to unwind the lower-priority order automatically while logging the full rationale.
  \item As its core innovation, the engine continuously analyzes its own conflict history to surface specific, evidence-based channel buffer suggestions on a unified live profit and stock dashboard, keeping a human operator in final control of every financial decision.
\end{itemize}

\section{Goals and Objectives}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Real-Time Multi-Channel Synchronization:} Eliminate the 15--30 minute polling blind spot by ingesting real-time sale, return, restock, and cancellation events via an Nginx-balanced cluster of three Node.js + Express webhook intake nodes.
  \item \textbf{Zero Duplicate Deductions (Idempotency):} Guarantee at-most-once stock deduction for retried channel notifications by enforcing atomic idempotency key checks in a fast Redis cache prior to queue insertion.
  \item \textbf{Crash-Resilient Event Durability:} Ensure zero event loss during traffic spikes or process failures by persisting validated events into a durable RabbitMQ message queue and an append-only PostgreSQL event store.
  \item \textbf{Margin-Aware Priority Conflict Resolution:} Detect concurrent stock-out race conditions deterministically and resolve conflicts by comparing net profit margin, customer lifetime importance, and channel SLA penalties rather than relying on naive first-come-first-served rejection.
  \item \textbf{Unified Auditability and Profit Visibility:} Provide a single live HTML/JS seller dashboard displaying current stock, full immutable event history, order-level net profit, and top customer behavior from the same underlying event stream.
  \item \textbf{Self-Improving Human-in-the-Loop Recommendations:} Analyze historical conflict frequencies per channel and SKU to surface actionable buffer allocation suggestions that require explicit human approval before taking effect.
\end{itemize}

\section{Relevant Mathematics Associated with the Project}
\label{sec:math}
Let the Multi-Channel Inventory Reconciliation System be formally modeled as an algebraic 7-tuple:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S} = \langle \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{E}, \Sigma, \Phi, \mathcal{C}_{\text{success}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{failure}} \rangle
\end{equation}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Input ($\mathcal{I}$):} A stream of asynchronous webhook notifications arriving from $M$ independent sales channels $\mathcal{CH} = \{\text{Amazon},\allowbreak \text{Flipkart},\allowbreak \text{Website},\allowbreak \text{POS}\}$. Each incoming notification is a tuple:
  \begin{equation}
  e_k = (\text{id}_k, s_k, c_k, \tau_k, \Delta q_k, m_k, u_k, t_k)
  \end{equation}
  where $\text{id}_k \in \Sigma^*$ is the unique channel notification identifier, $s_k \in \mathcal{SKU}$ is the product SKU, $c_k \in \mathcal{CH}$ is the originating channel, $\tau_k \in \{\text{SALE},\allowbreak \text{RETURN},\allowbreak \text{RESTOCK},\allowbreak \text{CANCEL},\allowbreak \text{UNWIND}\}$ is the event type, $\Delta q_k \in \mathbb{Z}$ is the signed stock change ($\Delta q_k < 0$ for sales, $\Delta q_k > 0$ for restocks/returns), $m_k \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is the net profit margin of the order, $u_k \in [0, 1]$ is the normalized customer importance score, and $t_k \in \mathbb{R}_+$ is the event timestamp.

  \item \textbf{Output ($\mathcal{O}$):}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Reconciled available stock projection $Q_t(s)$ for every $s \in \mathcal{SKU}$ broadcast to all channels $c \in \mathcal{CH}$.
    \item Conflict resolution decision $d_k \in \{\text{COMMIT}, \text{UNWIND}(o_j)\}$ whenever a race condition occurs.
    \item Live profitability metrics $\Pi(s, c)$ and human-in-the-loop safety stock buffer recommendation $B_{\text{rec}}(s, c) \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$.
  \end{enumerate}

  \item \textbf{Mathematical Formulation (Idempotency \& Event Sourcing State Fold):}
  Let $\mathcal{D}_{k-1} = \{\text{id}_1, \text{id}_2, \dots, \text{id}_{k-1}\}$ denote the set of processed event keys stored in Redis. The idempotency filter function $f_{\text{dedup}}$ is defined as:
  \begin{equation}
  \mathbb{I}_{\text{new}}(e_k) = \begin{cases} 1, & \text{if } \text{id}_k \notin \mathcal{D}_{k-1} \\ 0, & \text{if } \text{id}_k \in \mathcal{D}_{k-1} \text{ (duplicate webhook ignored)} \end{cases}
  \end{equation}
  Instead of overwriting a mutable variable, the canonical stock $Q_t(s)$ at discrete step $t$ is computed as the fold over the immutable PostgreSQL event ledger $\mathcal{L}_t(s)$:
  \begin{equation}
  Q_t(s) = Q_0(s) + \sum_{e_k \in \mathcal{L}_t(s)} \mathbb{I}_{\text{new}}(e_k) \cdot \Delta q_k
  \end{equation}

  \item \textbf{Conflict Detection and Priority-Based Resolution Formulation:}
  A stock-out conflict occurs at event $e_k$ if the tentative post-event stock satisfies:
  \begin{equation}
  \tilde{Q}_t(s) = Q_{t-1}(s) + \Delta q_k < 0
  \end{equation}
  Let $\mathcal{W}(s, \Delta t)$ be the set of concurrent contending orders within the race window $\Delta t$ for the last available unit(s) of SKU $s$. For each contending order $o_i \in \mathcal{W}(s, \Delta t)$, the Python conflict engine evaluates the composite retention utility score $U(o_i)$:
  \begin{equation}
  U(o_i) = w_1 \cdot \widehat{m}(o_i) + w_2 \cdot u(o_i) + w_3 \cdot \psi(c_i)
  \end{equation}
  where $\widehat{m}(o_i) \in [0,1]$ is the normalized net profit margin, $u(o_i) \in [0,1]$ is the customer loyalty/repeat-buyer score, $\psi(c_i) \in [0,1]$ is the channel SLA penalty weight, and $w_1 + w_2 + w_3 = 1$. The order selected for automatic unwinding and customer notification is the one minimizing retention utility:
  \begin{equation}
  o^*_{\text{unwind}} = \arg\min_{o_i \in \mathcal{W}(s, \Delta t)} U(o_i)
  \end{equation}
  Upon unwinding $o^*_{\text{unwind}}$, a compensating event $e_{\text{comp}}$ with $\Delta q = +|q(o^*_{\text{unwind}})|$ is appended to $\mathcal{L}_t(s)$, restoring $Q_t(s) \ge 0$ while preserving the full audit history.

  \item \textbf{Evidence-Based Buffer Recommendation Formulation:}
  Over an observation horizon $T$ (e.g., 30 days), let $N_{\text{conflict}}(s, c)$ be the number of lost-margin conflict cancellations attributed to channel $c$ for SKU $s$. When $N_{\text{conflict}}(s, c) \ge \theta_{\text{min}}$, the system generates a human-reviewable buffer suggestion:
  \begin{equation}
  B_{\text{rec}}(s, c) = \left\lceil \gamma \cdot \frac{N_{\text{conflict}}(s, c)}{T} \cdot \bar{q}_{\text{race}}(s, c) \right\rceil
  \end{equation}
  Subject to the strict human-governance invariant: active channel buffer $B_{\text{active}}(s, c)$ is updated to $B_{\text{rec}}(s, c)$ if and only if $\text{HumanApproval}(B_{\text{rec}}) = \text{TRUE}$.

  \item \textbf{Objects, Morphisms, and Functional Relations:}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Objects:} \texttt{RawWebhookPayload}, \texttt{VerifiedEvent}, \texttt{LedgerEntry}, \texttt{StockProjection}, \texttt{ConflictResolutionRecord}, \texttt{BufferRecommendation}.
    \item \textit{Morphisms:} $\phi_{\text{intake}}: \texttt{RawWebhookPayload} \rightarrow \texttt{VerifiedEvent}$ (via Redis deduplication); $\phi_{\text{append}}: \texttt{VerifiedEvent} \rightarrow \texttt{LedgerEntry}$ (via RabbitMQ and PostgreSQL); $\phi_{\text{fold}}: \mathcal{P}(\texttt{LedgerEntry}) \rightarrow \texttt{StockProjection}$.
    \item \textit{Overloading:} The event fold operator $\oplus$ is overloaded across event types (\texttt{SALE} decrements available stock and increments gross revenue; \texttt{RETURN} increments inspectable stock and adjusts realized margin; \texttt{UNWIND} restores stock and records conflict mitigation cost).
  \end{itemize}

  \item \textbf{Success Conditions ($\mathcal{C}_{\text{success}}$):}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $\forall s \in \mathcal{SKU}$, reconciled stock $Q_t(s) \ge 0$ after conflict resolution.
    \item Zero duplicate stock deductions for identical $\text{id}_k$ across all 3 intake nodes.
    \item $100\%$ of state transitions are reconstructible from the PostgreSQL event log.
