\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Project Idea}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Centralized Event-Sourced Truth:} Modern multi-channel sellers list identical products simultaneously across Amazon, Flipkart, their own direct-to-consumer (D2C) website, and physical shop counters. Our project builds a single, centralized reconciliation engine that every sales channel reports to in real time.
  \item \textbf{Recording Every Change Instead of Overwriting Numbers:} Rather than storing a single mutable field such as \texttt{current\_stock = 10}, our engine permanently records every individual inventory mutation as it happens—every sale, customer return, warehouse restock, and order cancellation. Current stock at any instant is simply the deterministic sum of everything that has ever occurred.
  \item \textbf{Six-Stage Resilient Pipeline:}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Stage 1 — Multi-Channel Webhook Intake:} Whenever a sale occurs on Amazon, Flipkart, the seller's website, or the physical POS counter, the channel dispatches a webhook notification through Nginx to one of three horizontally scaled Node.js + Express intake nodes.
    \item \textit{Stage 2 — Instant Duplicate Check:} Before processing, the intake node queries a high-speed Redis cache to verify whether this exact notification ID has already been seen. Duplicate retries are acknowledged and ignored immediately, preventing double stock deductions.
    \item \textit{Stage 3 — Durable Queueing and Permanent Logging:} Verified unique events are published to a durable RabbitMQ queue and appended to PostgreSQL so that even if a server crashes mid-operation, zero events are ever lost.
    \item \textit{Stage 4 — Deterministic Stock Recalculation:} A Python worker consumes the queued event, folds the updated stock count from the immutable event log, and checks whether the resulting stock would drop below zero.
    \item \textit{Stage 5 — Smart Priority-Based Conflict Resolution:} When two channels sell the final unit within milliseconds of each other, the engine does not blindly reject an order or let stock go negative. It evaluates which order is more costly to lose (weighing profit margin and customer importance), recommends or executes an automatic unwind of the lower-priority order, notifies the customer immediately, and permanently logs the exact reason.
    \item \textit{Stage 6 — Unified Live Profit Dashboard \& Self-Improving Advisor:} The exact same event ledger powers a live HTML/JS seller dashboard displaying real net profit per order, top customers, full audit trails, and proactive, evidence-based buffer suggestions that a human seller can approve or reject with one click.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\section{Motivation of the Project}
Multi-channel commerce is essential for modern retail growth, yet sellers operating across two or more platforms routinely suffer from \textbf{overselling}—promising a unit to a customer that has already been sold elsewhere seconds earlier, forcing order cancellations, marketplace penalties, refunds, and reputational damage. Our investigation identified five root causes why existing affordable systems fail to solve this problem:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Slow Periodic Syncing (15--30 Minute Blind Spots):} Most entry-level tools poll channels every 15 to 30 minutes and push batch stock updates. Any unit sold during the interval between two sync cycles remains completely invisible to other channels until the next cron job runs.
  \item \textbf{Concurrent Race Conditions:} Even tools that attempt instant webhook updates fail when two sales arrive at the exact same moment. Both channels read \texttt{stock = 1} simultaneously, both believe they are safe to fulfill, and both sell the same physical unit twice.
  \item \textbf{Duplicate Webhook Notifications:} Marketplaces frequently resend the same ``sale occurred'' webhook if the seller's endpoint responds slightly slowly due to network jitter. Without strict idempotency handling, the same real sale is deducted twice, creating phantom stock-outs.
  \item \textbf{No Intelligent Decision When Stock Runs Out:} Basic platforms either silently reject valid high-margin orders on a first-come-first-served basis or allow stock counts to drift into negative numbers without business logic.
  \item \textbf{Zero Audit History:} Because traditional systems overwrite the stock integer in place, when stock inevitably becomes inaccurate, the seller has no permanent ledger to inspect \textit{why} or \textit{when} the discrepancy happened.
\end{itemize}

\section{Literature Survey}
To position our contribution accurately and honestly within both academic literature and commercial industry platforms, we evaluated ten foundational research papers (detailed in Section \ref{sec:survey}) alongside a rigorous gap analysis across the three dominant categories of commercial tools currently available to merchants:
