\chapter{Problem Definition and scope}

\section{Problem Statement}
Four or more independent sales channels (Amazon, Flipkart, direct D2C website, and physical POS counter), which cannot communicate with each other directly or instantaneously, must behave as though they share a single, trustworthy, real-time inventory count. Whenever network latency, webhook retries, or simultaneous last-unit purchases cause temporary disagreement across channels, the system must automatically detect the conflict, deduplicate retried alerts, resolve stock-outs using business-priority metrics (profit margin and customer importance), maintain an immutable audit trail of every state change, and proactively recommend evidence-based buffer rules to a human operator without ever making autonomous financial policy changes behind the scenes.

\subsection{Goals and objectives}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Input Specification \& Validation:} Accept JSON webhook payloads over HTTPS from multiple channels containing \texttt{event\_id}, \texttt{channel\_id}, \texttt{sku\_id}, \texttt{event\_type} (\texttt{SALE}, \texttt{RETURN}, \texttt{RESTOCK}, \texttt{CANCEL}), \texttt{quantity}, \texttt{unit\_price}, \texttt{cost\_price}, \texttt{channel\_fee}, \texttt{customer\_id}, and \texttt{timestamp}.
  \item \textbf{Output Specification:} Emit (a) real-time HTTP 200 idempotency acknowledgments within $<25$ ms, (b) updated available stock counts pushed to connected channel adapters, (c) automated order-unwind and customer-notification triggers on stock-out conflicts, and (d) live JSON/WebSocket streams to the seller dashboard displaying net profit, audit history, and human-approval buffer recommendations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Statement of scope}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{In-Scope Capabilities:}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Load-balanced multi-node webhook ingestion using Nginx and three Node.js + Express instances.
    \item Sub-millisecond duplicate webhook suppression using Redis atomic keys with configurable TTL.
    \item Crash-resilient asynchronous queuing via RabbitMQ durable exchanges and queues.
    \item Append-only PostgreSQL event store recording every inventory mutation with full causality metadata.
    \item Python-based stock projection fold and priority-weighted conflict resolution engine.
    \item Historical conflict pattern analyzer that surfaces human-approved channel buffer suggestions.
    \item Live HTML/JS seller dashboard for stock monitoring, order-level profit analytics, audit trail inspection, and one-click approval/rejection of buffer recommendations.
    \item Reproducible multi-container deployment via Docker Compose.
  \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Out-of-Scope Boundaries:}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Physical warehouse robotics, barcode hardware manufacturing, or third-party logistics (3PL) fleet routing.
    \item Autonomous modification of pricing or safety stock buffers without explicit human approval (by strict design, the system never makes unapproved financial policy changes).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

\section{Major Constraints}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{External Marketplace Webhook Retry Semantics:} Platforms like Amazon and Flipkart enforce strict webhook timeout thresholds (typically 3--5 seconds) and automatically resend notifications upon timeout; therefore, intake nodes must respond asynchronously before heavy database conflict resolution completes.
  \item \textbf{CAP Theorem \& Eventual Channel Propagation:} Because external marketplaces cannot participate in a blocking two-phase commit (2PC) transaction with a seller's private server, transient race windows of 50--500 ms are physically unavoidable and must be handled via post-detection compensating unwind workflows.
  \item \textbf{Human Governance Constraint:} All automated recommendations generated by the conflict-history analyzer must remain in a \texttt{PENDING\_APPROVAL} state until an authenticated human operator explicitly approves or rejects them.
\end{itemize}

\section{Methodologies of Problem solving and efficiency issues}
A multi-channel inventory synchronization problem can be approached through three distinct architectural methodologies, each exhibiting markedly different performance and reliability characteristics:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Approach 1: Periodic Polling with Mutable State Overwrite (Traditional Basic Tools):} A cron job queries each channel every 15--30 minutes and executes \texttt{UPDATE products SET stock = X}. While simple to implement, its worst-case synchronization blind spot is $O(T_{\text{poll}})$ (up to 30 minutes), it suffers from lost updates under concurrent writes, and it destroys historical auditability.
  \item \textbf{Approach 2: Synchronous Distributed Locking / Two-Phase Commit (2PC):} Attempting to lock stock across all external channels before confirming any sale. In practice, third-party marketplaces (Amazon, Flipkart) do not expose distributed lock primitives to merchant servers, and synchronous cross-network locking degrades throughput and availability under the CAP theorem whenever any single channel API experiences latency.
  \item \textbf{Approach 3: Asynchronous Event Sourcing + Redis Idempotency + Priority Compensating Resolution (Proposed Methodology):} Decouples intake ($O(1)$ Redis deduplication + RabbitMQ enqueue in $<15$ ms) from sequential per-SKU event folding and priority-based conflict resolution in Python. This achieves high ingestion throughput across 3 Node.js nodes, $100\%$ crash durability, complete auditability in PostgreSQL, and optimal economic retention when simultaneous last-unit race conditions occur.
\end{itemize}

\section{Outcome}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A fully containerized, eight-layer distributed reconciliation engine orchestrated via Docker Compose.
  \item Elimination of duplicate webhook stock deductions and silent negative-stock states across four concurrent sales channels.
  \item A unified live seller web dashboard providing real-time stock visibility, complete event-sourced audit logs, order-level net profit tracking, and human-approved buffer recommendations.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications}
