\chapter{Detailed Design Document using Appendix A and B}

\section{Introduction}
This chapter specifies the detailed architectural, data, and component design of the \textbf{Multi-Channel Inventory Reconciliation Engine}, translating the SRS requirements into concrete microservices, database schemas, and object-oriented modules.

\section{Architectural Design}
The system follows an eight-layer containerized microservices architecture orchestrated via Docker Compose (shown in Figure \ref{fig:arch-dig}):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Traffic Distribution Layer (Nginx):} Acts as the single ingress gateway, distributing incoming webhook bursts across the three intake nodes using round-robin/least-connections load balancing with automatic health checks.
  \item \textbf{Webhook Intake Layer (3x Node.js + Express Nodes):} Stateless high-concurrency HTTP servers that validate payload schemas and coordinate fast deduplication.
  \item \textbf{Duplicate Check \& Fast Cache Layer (Redis):} Executes atomic \texttt{SETNX} operations on incoming event IDs to guarantee that retried channel notifications are filtered out in sub-millisecond time.
  \item \textbf{Durable Event Queue \& Dead-Letter Queue Layer (RabbitMQ + DLQ):} Persists accepted events to disk-backed queues so that no sale event is lost even if downstream workers restart, while routing malformed or unprocessable events to a Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) for safe inspection.
  \item \textbf{Event Processing \& Conflict Logic Layer (Python Worker):} Sequentially folds SKU events, detects negative-stock race conditions, evaluates order retention scores based on profit margin and customer value, triggers automated unwinds, and analyzes conflict history to generate buffer suggestions.
  \item \textbf{Notification \& Channel Sync Service (Email/SMS/API):} Dispatches instant customer refund and apology notifications when an order is unwound and pushes live reconciled stock counts back to Amazon, Flipkart, the seller website, and the physical POS counter.
  \item \textbf{Permanent Event Log \& Stock Data Layer (PostgreSQL):} Stores the immutable event ledger, conflict audit trail, customer profitability metrics, and buffer recommendations.
  \item \textbf{Seller Dashboard Layer (Web UI — HTML/JS):} Displays live stock across channels, real profit per order, full conflict explanations, and one-click human approval/rejection controls for suggested channel buffers.
  \item \textbf{Packaging Layer (Docker Compose):} Encapsulates all services, networks, and persistent volumes for reproducible one-command deployment anywhere.
\end{enumerate}

