\subsection{Temporary data structure}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{RabbitMQ In-Flight Message Frame:} Persistent AMQP message stored in the \texttt{inventory\_reconciliation\_queue} until explicitly acknowledged (\texttt{basic\_ack}) by the Python worker after PostgreSQL commit.
  \item \textbf{Contending Order Window Buffer:} In-memory Python priority heap comparing concurrent orders for the same SKU during conflict resolution.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Database description}
PostgreSQL 16 serves as the primary ACID relational and event-store database. By combining an append-only \texttt{inventory\_events} table with indexed \texttt{sku\_projections}, \texttt{conflict\_audit\_log}, and \texttt{buffer\_recommendations} tables, the database supports both $O(1)$ current-state lookups and $100\%$ historical replayability.

\section{Component Design}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{\texttt{WebhookIntakeController} (Node.js):} Handles HTTP POST requests, verifies channel HMAC signatures, checks Redis idempotency, and publishes to RabbitMQ.
  \item \textbf{\texttt{EventSourcedLedger} (Python):} Appends verified events to PostgreSQL and computes current stock as the cumulative sum of event deltas.
  \item \textbf{\texttt{ConflictResolutionEngine} (Python):} Detects when concurrent sales push stock below zero, ranks competing orders by net margin and customer value, generates a compensating \texttt{UNWIND} event for the lower-priority order, and dispatches a customer notification.
  \item \textbf{\texttt{AdaptiveRecommendationAdvisor} (Python):} Periodically aggregates \texttt{conflict\_audit\_log} entries over a rolling 30-day window and creates human-reviewable buffer suggestions when a channel repeatedly causes lost-margin cancellations.
\end{itemize}

