\chapter{Laboratory assignments on Project Quality and Reliability Testing of Project Design}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Divide and Conquer Strategy for Concurrent Processing:}
  The workload is partitioned horizontally at the ingress tier across three stateless Node.js intake nodes (handling concurrent HTTP parsing and HMAC verification in parallel) and partitioned logically by \texttt{sku\_id} at the Python worker tier so that stock projections for distinct SKUs execute concurrently without cross-SKU contention.
  \item \textbf{Functional Dependency \& Reliability Modeling:}
  Let $R_{\text{nginx}} = 0.999$, each intake node $R_{\text{node}} = 0.99$ (3 nodes in parallel redundancy), $R_{\text{redis}} = 0.999$, $R_{\text{mq}} = 0.999$, and $R_{\text{pg}} = 0.999$. Because the 3 Node.js intake nodes operate in parallel active-active redundancy, the intake tier reliability is:
  \begin{equation}
  R_{\text{intake}} = 1 - (1 - 0.99)^3 = 1 - 10^{-6} = 0.999999
  \end{equation}
  Combined with RabbitMQ durable disk persistence and PostgreSQL ACID logging, the system achieves high end-to-end event capture reliability.
  \item \textbf{Black-Box Functional Test Partitioning:}
  Equivalence classes tested include: (1) Valid unique sale event with $Q_t \ge 0$, (2) Valid unique sale event triggering race conflict $Q_t < 0$, (3) Exact duplicate \texttt{event\_id} replay within TTL window, (4) Restock/Return positive $\Delta q$ recovery event, and (5) Human approval vs. rejection of generated buffer recommendations.
\end{itemize}

